# Jira batch updates — no personal watcher

When updating Jira issues in a **batch process** (scripts, automation, multi-issue `--execute` runs):

## Default: do not watch issues under nwilker's account

Jira **auto-adds the acting user as a watcher** when they edit an issue or add a comment. A batch of hundreds of updates will flood `nwilker@redhat.com` with watches and notifications.

## Before `--execute` on any batch Jira script

1. **Prefer the VME Automation Bot** (`--bot`, `.env_vme_automation_bot`) so changes and watches are attributed to `vme-automation-bot@redhat.com`, not the personal account.
2. If the run must use **personal credentials** (`.env_wilker_jira`):
   - **Ask and get explicit confirmation** before executing.
   - Tell the user that Jira will auto-add them as a watcher on every touched issue unless mitigated.
   - After the run, **offer to remove nwilker as watcher** from all updated issue keys (see `cnv_unwatch_personal_run.log` / DELETE `/rest/api/3/issue/{key}/watchers?accountId=...`).

## Dry-run first

Always run dry-run (default) and show counts before `--execute`, unless the user explicitly skips preview.

## Scripts in this repo

| Script | Bot flag |
|--------|----------|
| `copy_components_from_parent_cnv.py` | `--execute --bot` |
| `copy_cipoe_links_to_virtstrat.py` | `--execute --bot` |

## Do not

- Run large batch `--execute` under personal credentials without confirmation.
- Assume dry-run or MCP updates avoid watcher side effects — they do not.
